00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.640
And now one thing I want to
cover, also open source,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.750
is an experiment that we
are trying out that we want

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.640
to see how you guys like it.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.980
So, this is
Intelligent Terminal.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.380
And it kind of came from,
you know, we're working

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.870
with all these agents, but
when you go into Copilot CLI,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.100
it takes over your
whole terminal.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.090
So, it would be really nice if
you had a companion working

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.550
with you, so you could
still stay in your prompt,

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.520
but then have an agent
helping you along the way.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.943
So, here's a regex command that

00:04:39.967 --> 00:04:42.390
I asked an agent
to write for me,

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.290
because I don't know regex,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.690
but I also asked it
to make it not work.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.200
And the agent has detected
that it doesn't work.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.130
This is meant to be like a
password verification regex.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.070
And the agent is now thinking
about how to fix this.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:01.500
And it should give me a solution
to create a better regex.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.360
So, it's not giving me
a command specifically

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.790
because it doesn't know
my desired outcome.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.710
Do I want to check for
an uppercase letter?

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.200
Do I want to check for
a unique character?

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.550
So, I can work with the agent to
come up with a better syntax

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.410
of regex without having
to leave the terminal.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.400
And what's really cool is

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.460
when you first get Intelligent
Terminal, it's going to ask you,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.770
like, hey, which agent do
you want to work with?

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.210
I only have GitHub
Copilot installed,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.400
because I'm a
GitHub Copilot fan.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.240
But you can add in Claude,
Codex, Open Code, anything.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.410
Once you install
on your machine,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.610
it should automatically
get picked

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.620
up by Intelligent Terminal.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.080
And you can pick your
preferred model as well.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.730
So, this is available today.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.260
We want to get your
feedback on this, like,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.800
how does this agentic pain feel
when you're working with it?

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.510
What kind of things
do you want to change

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.040
or have added to the experience?

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.060
We want to know.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.560
So, this is open source.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.470
It's on GitHub, published, I
think, this morning as well.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.990
Everything is fresh
this morning.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.580
So yeah, go ahead
and try this out,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.320
and let us know how you like it.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.120
But since we're on the
topic of terminal things,

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.720
I want to hand it over to Craig
to show a little bit more.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:04.050
Craig Loewen: Yeah.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.150
So, let's take a look at my
machine, and we're going

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.980
to take a look at
WSL containers,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.860
also freshly announced
this morning,

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.720
and something we're
really excited about.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.880
My goal today is
to convince you,

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.740
if you have never used
containers before,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.170
why you might want to
get started using them,

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.990
and show you how you can do
all this with WSL containers.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.490
So, we've added a new binary to
WSL called WSL C. I can go ahead

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.110
and run this, and I can run tons

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.540
of different Linux
container commands.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:35.370
So, Linux container is basically
just a very nice little package

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.150
where I can run arbitrary Linux
binaries or distributions.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.550
In this case, I just ran Ubuntu.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.320
I ran the latest
version of Ubuntu.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.160
But if I'm more of a Debian fan,
I can very easily just go here

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.410
and say, hey, give me the
latest version of Debian,

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.380
and then it will be able
to go pull it down for me.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.450
Even though I didn't have that
downloaded, it pulled it down

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.550
and ran that, so I can run

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.950
that directly here
inside of my terminal.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.870
Where containers get
really powerful,

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.390
and where this really
shows for WSL container,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:07.140
is you can use this to build
powerful Linux environments

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.180
that you can share to
the cloud or locally.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.390
And now this all works just
directly as part of WSL.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.250
On top of that,
we have container

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.900
as an alias that's built in.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.290
So, you can use either WSL C or
container, whichever is more

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:20.850
to your heart's desire.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:24.630
So, if I go to this file here,
I have a "Container" folder,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.480
and in it, I have some
different container files.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.380
And so, what I can do is
actually build this image.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:37.080
So, I'm going to build a service
directly inside of my terminal.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.740
So, it's going to go ahead,
grab that image, build it.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.050
I actually ran this before,
so everything was cached.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:46.110
And if we go ahead and take a
look at that container file

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.270
with edit, great editor tool.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.060
If you haven't seen this in the
command line, it's beautiful.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.270
Works with your mouse, which I
-- probably my favorite part.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.670
And if I go and take
a look at this,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:01.320
I can see I'm grabbing Python
3.13, and then I am going

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.600
and running a bunch of
different Linux commands to set

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.790
up a specific Linux environment.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.370
Now that I've built that
container, let's go ahead

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.160
and take a look
at it in my list.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:14.190
So, I just ran WSL C, Image
LS, and then I piped it

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.770
to Grep and ran MarkItDown.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:17.760
Well, hold on.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.320
Piping it to Grep is pretty cool

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.010
because we have just released
Coreutils, which is a new way

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:27.030
to run a ton of different Linux
core utilities all directly

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:27.960
on Windows.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.320
So, things like Grep are now
available as part of that.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.870
I can even go ahead
and take a look at --

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.540
here, I'm looking at that list.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.660
And then I pipe this to WC,
which counts the word count.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.680
It's telling me there's 165
different tools inside of this.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.830
So, let's take a
look at the list,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:50.400
things like test.exe, tail.exe.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.760
I'm personally really
excited about env.exe,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.220
which shows your
environment variables,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.130
because I only really know
how to do that in Bash,

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.050
speaking from
personal experience.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.430
And it's just a super
awesome and powerful way to,

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.310
if you're a big Linux fan, like
I am, and you're a big fan

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.020
of bringing those
worlds together,

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.900
they can just coexist so nicely.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.010
And so, let's kind of go
back to our example.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:19.100
And I am going to run

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.910
that MarkItDown
service, which I built.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.070
And so, I can go
and run that here.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.800
And so, I took that
container that I defined

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.700
in that container file, and
then now I'm starting it up.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.730
What you'll notice is
that I use this dash p

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.900
to say please take port
8000 in the container

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.360
and forward that onto Windows.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.450
And so, now when I have
that, and I go to Edge,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:50.520
and I go to local host 8000,
I get what that container is.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.480
So, that container is defining a
very neat little web app for me.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.390
That web app allows me to
go in and take any folder.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.960
So, let's go and
find my samples,

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.510
which I have very conveniently
placed about 30 folders down.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.500
Just 29 more.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.760
Just 39 more.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.290
All right, here we go.

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:16.460
So, there we go.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.270
And then I can drag
and drop this in.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:22.440
All this does is it takes any
PDF file or any file in here,

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:26.340
like dot doc x. So,
took this complex pdf,

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:29.900
and then it converted that
into markdown, and it shows me

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:31.430
that on the left
and on the right.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:35.480
So, that could be my full
website contained right there.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.110
I was able to go run that.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.210
And so, if that
didn't convince you,

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:42.510
I think the last thing I'd
really show is you can do things

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.240
like light your whole
terminal on fire

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.690
by running different containers,
so that someone built that,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.180
that you can go run with
just one command line,

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:53.400
a super powerful tool.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.110
And I think it begs the question

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.920
of why did we build a
Linux container CLI,

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.050
when there are other
awesome CLI tools,

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.860
like there's Podman Desktop,
Docker Desktop, Rancher Desktop.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.290
And the answer is these
are all great tools,

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.980
but we actually also built
an API for this as well.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.570
And we're going to be
showing that in Part 2

