00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:57.390
So, if you ever work on, say,
performance on Windows,

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.750
you must have heard about a
profile-guided optimization

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.430
called PGO there.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:05.730
The basic idea is just to
collect the actual, say,

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.760
executable running and sample
the data, where it goes,

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:11.790
which part will be executed, and
feed that into the compiler,

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:14.670
so that compiler would do a
better job about optimizing it

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.950
when you're spitting out
the binary code set.

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:20.100
So, we've heard, say, good
performance number improvements

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.560
from the PGO, say 5%, 10%.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.600
The benefit is very real,

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.780
but the cost is a little bit
hindering the adoption among the

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:29.730
ISV, like you hear.

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:32.070
The two major things that we
heard about is that, one,

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:33.690
it requires some
instrumentation,

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:35.910
which means some extra
code in your app there.

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:37.500
You need to do that.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:40.080
Second, when you run that,
when you build that,

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.960
there will be an extra fork
in your build pipeline there.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:47.040
And not everyone is a fan
about extra things to mess

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.470
with your build
pipeline on that one.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.570
And last, but not the least,
when you run the instrument

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.450
to build, usually,
it's very slow.

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:57.990
So, it's not actually mimicking
what the end user will see,

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:03.120
making the quality of a profile
questionable on that one.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:07.260
So, this is where the SPG,
or the sample-based PGO,

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:08.820
come in to rescue you.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.340
Instead of using the app
code instrumentation,

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:15.060
