00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:05.280
There are a lot of customers who
want to be able to use the power

00:34:05.280 --> 00:34:08.940
of Linux or don't even know that
they need the power of Linux,

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:10.290
and they don't want
to know about it.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:12.630
For example, my mom and
dad don't need to know

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:15.900
that they're doing some great
Linux stuff in the background.

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:17.790
They just need to
run powerful apps.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.370
So, let's take a look
at that same receipt

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.230
that I put in earlier.

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:23.190
Well, here we go.

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:27.540
It goes in the Windows app and
does the exact same rendering,

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.310
which is pretty great.

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:30.870
And so, I've made this log here

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:35.790
that would show how quickly
things start up or shut down.

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:38.730
If I go and run this app
again, I'll watch that,

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:39.900
and you can see the speed

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:41.460
that we're starting
up that container.

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:42.630
It basically takes about two

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:45.420
and a half seconds is how
long we're benchmarking

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:46.890
from a cold start.

00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:50.460
And so, it's going and
initializing the session.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:52.920
And then it will start
up the container.

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:54.330
And then it started.

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:57.390
And then now it's running
the Linux code inside of it.

00:34:57.390 --> 00:35:00.090
So, then it executed
my Python service.

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:04.890
And all of this is powered by,
if I go to my task manager,

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:08.430
I can see that I have
a new process here.

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:12.390
That MarkItDown service
runs in its own WSL VM.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.220
So, it's the exact
same VM technology

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:16.220
that powers your WSL distros.

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:18.650
We're now applying it to Linux
containers running as part

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:20.450
of a Windows application.

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:23.780
So, each Windows app
gets its own WSL VM.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:25.040
And you can run as
many containers

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:26.670
as you want inside of it.

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:27.600
And this wouldn't be a demo

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.460
without taking a
look at the code.

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:33.150
So, we for sure have to go see
how we're actually doing this.

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:37.080
And as you can see, it's just
some C# code that's not

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:37.860
too long.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.160
I'm able to go in
here and say, hey,

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:42.840
please start a new
session for me.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:45.210
That starts the virtual machine

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:48.060
and with these CPU
and memory count.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.130
And then I can start that.

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:51.540
I can pull a specific image.

00:35:51.540 --> 00:35:53.310
In this case, I'm
running that locally.

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:56.010
And where it's really powerful
is I can configure this

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.380
to have exactly what
options I need.

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:01.530
So, in this case, I said
I want only port 8000

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.900
to be mapped over,
so that I'm exposing

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:06.930
that API that I used before.

00:36:06.930 --> 00:36:08.460
And you can add
other options here,

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:11.910
like you can do volume mounting,
for example, to say, hey,

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.520
I want to be able to run

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:16.470
or access only specific
Windows folders.

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:17.730
Or you can even do
things like, hey,

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:19.920
I want to give this
access to my GPU.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:23.100
And so, super powerful
way that runs.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.320
And when it's all
closed and done,

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:27.060
all of those resources
are cleaned up.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:30.240
You can see the
VM has gone away.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:32.330
So, it works just
like any other app.

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:35.270
The last thing I'd want to
show is we're also improving

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:36.470
the ecosystem.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:39.590
So, we're really trying to make
this a very comprehensive

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:40.790
end-to-end story of developing

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:44.090
with containers wherever
you are, API or CLI.

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:47.130
So, we're doing fun things
like if you go to --

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.170
there's a project, lazywslc.

00:36:49.170 --> 00:36:53.640
You can get a very nice
TUI demo or TUI interface

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:54.900
for all of your containers.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.500
So, you can go ahead and take
this as a nice dashboard.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:00.570
On top of that, this
will also be supported

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:01.920
with dev containers.

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:04.830
So, if you do commands like
attach to running container,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.470
reopening container,
or use it in VS Code,

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:10.740
WSL container will be
supported there as well.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:15.090
And then last but not least,
for our WSL container demos,

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:17.100
we also wanted to highlight
an amazing partner

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:18.360
that we've been working with.

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:21.420
So, we've been working
with the MOONRAY team.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:24.810
They're a team that builds a
super powerful Linux-based

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.040
rendering engine.

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:28.230
And that is used to
render tons of movies

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.870
that you might be aware
of, like The Bad Guys 2

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:34.790
or The Wild Robot, which I'm a
big fan of, as I love robots.

00:37:34.790 --> 00:37:40.620
And so, this is a team that has
all of their code in Linux

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:43.710
and is open source, fully
open source, just like WSL.

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.600
And they want to bring
this awesome tool

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:48.480
to Windows users to easily use.

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:51.630
Well, how do you bridge that gap
and make it production ready?

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:54.390
That's where we've been able to
work together on taking a look

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:57.840
at the Windows subsystem
for Linux containers.

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:00.480
And so, I can go ahead and
take a quick look here.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:02.820
I have a demo app of their code.

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:06.030
So, I have the
WSLC-Moonray folder.

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:09.630
And I have built this
and created moonray.exe.

00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:12.720
So, that's very obviously
a Windows executable.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:16.050
And I'm also inputting
inputs to this.

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:17.640
And if you note the backslash,

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:20.820
these are definitely Windows
files that I'm giving in.

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.880
And these are Windows
files of .rdla,

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:25.830
which are basically
render inputs.

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:26.970
And then I'm asking it, hey,

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:30.000
could you please render
out this jpeg for me.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.670
And when I run this, it's
going to run exactly the same

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:36.120
as what I showed earlier,
just a command line version.

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:37.860
And I've shown that these --

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:41.610
I've kept in this debug
information here.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.920
So, you can see that that
was the machine starting.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.080
Now we have the actual
rendering happening.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:49.680
It's using up all of my CPU,
which is exactly what you want

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:52.740
to see when you're
doing a nice render.

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:56.280
And then once it's done, it
will perfectly shut down,

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:57.630
clean up the VM.

00:38:57.630 --> 00:38:59.460
And I can go ahead
and take a look

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:01.080
